WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:02.279
Will Gold remain an important form

00:00:02.280 --> 00:00:03.209
of money?

00:00:03.210 --> 00:00:05.609
Or are cryptocurrencies like Bitcoin

00:00:05.610 --> 00:00:07.199
set to overtake it?

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:09.419
That was the subject of a Soho forum

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:11.399
debate held on Tuesday, July

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:13.259
26, at the Mises

00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:15.659
Institute in Auburn, Alabama,

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:16.660
as part of Mises University

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:19.709
Week, an annual event attended

00:00:19.710 --> 00:00:21.659
by more than 80 students from

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:22.859
around the country.

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:24.809
Keith Weiner, CEO of Monetary

00:00:24.810 --> 00:00:27.059
Metals, defended the resolution,

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:29.039
"Gold will remain an important form

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:31.199
of money in the 21st century."

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:33.089
Weiner took the position that gold

00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:35.129
is poised to hold on to the monetary

00:00:35.130 --> 00:00:37.079
status it's enjoyed for the past

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:39.029
5000 years, and

00:00:39.030 --> 00:00:40.949
that its recent performance only

00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:42.209
confirms why.

00:00:42.210 --> 00:00:44.099
Pierre Rochard, co-host of the

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:46.559
Bitcoin for Advisers podcast,

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:48.479
took the negative, arguing that the

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:50.999
technological advantages of Bitcoin

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:52.979
will make it the preferred medium

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:54.929
of exchange in a post dollar

00:00:54.930 --> 00:00:55.859
world.

00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:57.332
This debate was moderated by Soho

00:00:57.333 --> 00:00:59.549
Forum director Gene Epstein.

00:01:04.370 --> 00:01:06.349
Without any further ado,

00:01:06.350 --> 00:01:08.569
I want to remind you of the

00:01:08.570 --> 00:01:09.589
resolution.

00:01:09.590 --> 00:01:11.809
Gold. Gold will remain

00:01:11.810 --> 00:01:13.669
an important form of money in the

00:01:13.670 --> 00:01:15.139
21st century.

00:01:15.140 --> 00:01:17.389
Please vote initially

00:01:17.390 --> 00:01:19.819
yes, no, or undecided

00:01:19.820 --> 00:01:21.769
on the resolution here to

00:01:21.770 --> 00:01:23.299
defend the resolution.

00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:24.589
Keith Wiener.

00:01:24.590 --> 00:01:25.970
Keith, please come to the stage.

00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:34.219
Taking.

00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:35.449
Taking the negative on the

00:01:35.450 --> 00:01:36.739
resolution.

00:01:36.740 --> 00:01:37.699
Pierre Rochard.

00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:39.319
Pierre, please come to the stage.

00:01:44.930 --> 00:01:46.309
Okay.

00:01:46.310 --> 00:01:48.139
I hope you've all voted yes or no

00:01:48.140 --> 00:01:49.969
or undecided on the resolution.

00:01:49.970 --> 00:01:52.159
Connor, please close

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:53.209
the voting.

00:01:53.210 --> 00:01:55.099
Keith, you have 15 minutes to

00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:56.299
defend the resolution.

00:01:56.300 --> 00:01:57.300
Take it away, Keith.

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:04.979
If this debate

00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:07.169
were about which asset

00:02:07.170 --> 00:02:09.119
is better at skyrocketing,

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:10.120
also crashing.

00:02:11.560 --> 00:02:13.539
Then I would shake my

00:02:13.540 --> 00:02:16.539
opponent's hand, concede

00:02:16.540 --> 00:02:18.699
and walk off the stage

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:20.829
because bitcoin is obviously

00:02:20.830 --> 00:02:23.439
superior at skyrocketing

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:24.440
and also crashing.

00:02:26.710 --> 00:02:28.270
However, that was not the debate.

00:02:29.290 --> 00:02:31.759
The debate is gold will remain money

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:32.889
in the important form of money in

00:02:32.890 --> 00:02:33.890
the 21st century.

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:37.229
Ladies and gentlemen, our

00:02:37.230 --> 00:02:38.230
esteemed moderators Gene Epstein.

00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:41.279
My honored opponent here,

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:42.280
Rochard.

00:02:43.220 --> 00:02:45.109
In a way, this debate is

00:02:45.110 --> 00:02:46.110
a bit unfair

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:48.589
because gold has been doing what it

00:02:48.590 --> 00:02:50.749
does for 5000 years,

00:02:50.750 --> 00:02:52.249
and the proposition is that it's

00:02:52.250 --> 00:02:53.539
going to continue doing for the next

00:02:53.540 --> 00:02:55.249
hundred years what it's been doing

00:02:55.250 --> 00:02:57.259
for 5000 years in

00:02:57.260 --> 00:02:58.639
a certain sense.

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:00.589
Good luck to debunking

00:03:00.590 --> 00:03:01.910
that or arguing against that.

00:03:03.330 --> 00:03:04.330
But

00:03:05.370 --> 00:03:06.449
there's a lot more to be said,

00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:08.489
obviously, about

00:03:08.490 --> 00:03:10.500
money and excuse me

00:03:12.450 --> 00:03:13.560
and how all this works.

00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:16.500
So.

00:03:18.310 --> 00:03:20.199
We live in a system where we have

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:22.119
a failing dollar.

00:03:22.120 --> 00:03:22.989
I don't think that's very

00:03:22.990 --> 00:03:24.849
controversial in this audience.

00:03:24.850 --> 00:03:26.709
Some audiences I speak to, you

00:03:26.710 --> 00:03:27.829
say that people like what?

00:03:27.830 --> 00:03:29.199
And then the room breaks into

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:30.609
pandemonium.

00:03:30.610 --> 00:03:33.399
But the dollar is designed.

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:35.169
This is a feature, not a bug.

00:03:35.170 --> 00:03:36.759
I'll keep referring to this concept

00:03:36.760 --> 00:03:38.259
of a feature, not a bug.

00:03:38.260 --> 00:03:41.199
The dollar is designed to go down.

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:43.179
The Fed on its Web site says

00:03:43.180 --> 00:03:45.339
2% per year, which they define

00:03:45.340 --> 00:03:47.289
as price stability.

00:03:47.290 --> 00:03:48.669
I wonder what Orwell would be saying

00:03:48.670 --> 00:03:49.670
about that.

00:03:50.790 --> 00:03:52.319
So it's designed to go down to a

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:53.429
steady rate.

00:03:53.430 --> 00:03:54.479
Why?

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:56.459
As a as a free boon to

00:03:56.460 --> 00:03:58.529
borrowers, if you borrow

00:03:58.530 --> 00:04:00.539
money and then the dollar's going

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:02.279
down, then what you owe the return

00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:04.619
of is falling in value.

00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:07.439
The longer the term of the loan,

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:09.569
the greater the fall, the easier

00:04:09.570 --> 00:04:11.129
the burden on the debtors.

00:04:11.130 --> 00:04:13.009
And that is how the system,

00:04:13.010 --> 00:04:15.119
if I can use the word rigged,

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:16.120
is rigged.

00:04:17.140 --> 00:04:19.389
Unfortunately, the dollar doesn't

00:04:19.390 --> 00:04:21.369
do as they would wish, doesn't

00:04:21.370 --> 00:04:23.139
do as their theory would predict,

00:04:23.140 --> 00:04:25.029
and it isn't steady

00:04:25.030 --> 00:04:26.169
or stable.

00:04:26.170 --> 00:04:28.119
It is not a simple mechanism for

00:04:28.120 --> 00:04:30.039
robbing Peter to pay Paul.

00:04:30.040 --> 00:04:31.179
Peter being the lender and the

00:04:31.180 --> 00:04:34.179
other. So every trade has two sides.

00:04:34.180 --> 00:04:35.649
And there's Peter over here is a

00:04:35.650 --> 00:04:37.359
saver who's lending in savings to

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:39.279
something and expecting to get that

00:04:39.280 --> 00:04:40.329
back with interest.

00:04:40.330 --> 00:04:42.369
Well, he's the one who's screwed

00:04:42.370 --> 00:04:43.809
when the dollar goes down.

00:04:43.810 --> 00:04:44.829
And then, Paul, over here, the

00:04:44.830 --> 00:04:46.449
borrower is the one who wins when

00:04:46.450 --> 00:04:48.219
the dollar goes down. Unfortunately

00:04:48.220 --> 00:04:50.259
for Paul,

00:04:50.260 --> 00:04:52.149
it isn't a steady,

00:04:52.150 --> 00:04:53.150
linear,

00:04:54.250 --> 00:04:56.739
monotonic, would be the term,

00:04:56.740 --> 00:04:58.629
progression of going down and

00:04:58.630 --> 00:05:00.669
has wicked volatility to it.

00:05:00.670 --> 00:05:01.779
And as we see right now, the

00:05:01.780 --> 00:05:04.029
dollar's going up, not down,

00:05:04.030 --> 00:05:05.229
which means the borrowers are

00:05:05.230 --> 00:05:07.089
finding that their burden of debt

00:05:07.090 --> 00:05:08.649
is increasing, not decreasing.

